vation only when the tissue to be spared is free of tumor.<sup>[[22]](#ref22)</sup>
 
-Vaginal complications after cystectomy are under-recognized. In a Medicare sample of 481 women 65 years or older undergoing cystectomy for any indication (83% with an incontinent conduit or catheterizable-channel diversion, not predominantly neobladder), 19.5% had at least one vaginal complication on record within 2 years, most often vaginal cuff dehiscence (10.2%); over the full study period, 21.2% of women were diagnosed with a vaginal complication and 26% of those received an intervention.<sup>[[42]](#ref42)</sup> A separate systematic review found neobladder-vaginal fistula, usually along the anterior vaginal wall at the neobladder-urethral anastomosis, in 3–6% of patients at higher-volume centers.<sup>[[43]](#ref43)</sup> These figures describe older, largely non-neobladder or mixed cohorts and do not estimate risk for a contemporary organ-sparing orthotopic reconstruction specifically.<sup>[[42]](#ref42)[[43]](#ref43)</sup>
