 The analogous functional, reconstructive-consequence consideration in men is nerve-sparing cystectomy, using the same neurovascular-bundle-preservation technique lateral to the prostatic capsule described for radical prostatectomy, to support erectile function recovery and continence with an orthotopic neobladder.<sup>[[39]](#ref39)</sup> Some centers have described prostate- or prostate-capsule-sparing cystectomy for the same functional goals, but this should be considered only in highly selected men because of the risk of missing incidental prostate adenocarcinoma or urothelial carcinoma involving the prostate: incidental prostate adenocarcinoma was found in 50/121 (41%) and 37.9% of 1,476 cystoprostatectomy specimens in the cited series (clinically significant in 8.3% of the larger cohort), and urothelial involvement of the prostate carries its own oncologic implications.<sup>[[40]](#ref40)[[41]](#ref41)</sup> The current AUA/ASCO/SUO muscle-invasive bladder cancer guideline frames sexual-function-preserving technique, like gynecologic organ preservation in women, as something to discuss with appropriately selected, motivated patients rather than a default approach.<sup>[[35]](#ref35)</sup>
 
